.TH MEMORY 9
.SH NAME
memory \- the physical allocator, page tables, the kernel heap
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import "kernel:mem"
.PP
.nf
.B "alloc_pages :: proc (count: int) -> (phys: uintptr, ok: bool)"
.B "alloc_pages_zeroed :: proc (count: int) -> (phys: uintptr, ok: bool)"
.B "alloc_page :: proc () -> (phys: uintptr, ok: bool)"
.B "free_pages :: proc (phys: uintptr, count: int)"
.B "free_page :: proc (phys: uintptr)"
.B "frame_share :: proc (phys: uintptr)"
.B "pmm_stats :: proc () -> Pmm_Stats"
.PP
.B "map_at :: proc (space: ^Address_Space, virt, phys: uintptr, flags: arch.Page_Flags, level: int) -> Error"
.B "unmap_page :: proc (space: ^Address_Space, virt: uintptr) -> bool"
.B "translate :: proc (space: ^Address_Space, virt: uintptr) -> (phys: uintptr, ok: bool)"
.B "permissions :: proc (space: ^Address_Space, virt: uintptr) -> (arch.Page_Flags, bool)"
.B "map_mmio :: proc (phys: uintptr, size: u64) -> (rawptr, Error)"
.B "kernel_address_space :: proc () -> ^Address_Space"
.B "phys_to_virt :: proc (phys: uintptr) -> rawptr"
.B "virt_to_phys :: proc (virt: rawptr) -> uintptr"
.B "vmm_stats :: proc () -> Vmm_Stats"
.PP
.B "alloc :: proc (size: int, align: int = MIN_ALIGN, zeroed := true) -> (rawptr, bool)"
.B "free :: proc (ptr: rawptr) -> bool"
.B "allocator :: proc () -> runtime.Allocator"
.B "heap_stats :: proc () -> Heap_Stats"
.B "live_objects :: proc (s: Heap_Stats) -> int"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Mem
is three layers, each built on the one below and each usable alone.
A
.B uintptr
in this package is always a physical address; a
.B rawptr
or a typed pointer is always virtual.
.B phys_to_virt
and
.B virt_to_phys
are the only bridge, through the higher-half direct map
.RB ( HHDM ).
Nothing in the package knows the boot protocol: a
.B Boot_Memory
built by
.B kernel/main.odin
out of whatever the bootloader reported is all it is told.
.SS The physical memory manager
.B pmm.odin
is one bit per page frame in a bitmap covering every frame from zero to
the top of allocatable memory, hole frames included, born taken and never
freed.
A bitmap rather than a free list: a free list lives inside the pages it
tracks, so a fault at the wrong moment corrupts the allocator itself, and
a contiguous run of frames, which the VMM needs for tables and the heap
needs for large objects, is a bitmap scan rather than a linear walk.
.B alloc_pages
returns a run of
.I count
physically contiguous frames;
.B alloc_page
is the one-frame case, and the
.B _zeroed
variants clear the pages before returning them.
.B free_pages
and
.B free_page
give frames back.
.B frame_share
raises a frame's holder count past one, for a copy-on-write reference; the
frame returns to the free list only on the last release.
.B Pmm_Stats
reports total, usable and free frame counts, a peak, the bitmap's own
size and location, and two counters that are never supposed to move:
.B double_frees
counts a release of an already-free frame, and
.B untracked_frees
counts a release of an address outside the bitmap.
Both are always a caller bug.
.SS The virtual memory manager
.B vmm.odin
is a radix tree of 512-entry page tables, indexed nine bits of virtual
address per level, with leaves permitted part way up the tree for a large
mapping.
The walk is written once, against
.IR arch (9)'s
encoding primitives, so it is not amd64-specific.
An
.B Address_Space
holds the physical address of its top-level table and a spinlock that
guards a walk against a concurrent one from another core; the lock is
held for one walk and never across a shootdown, which waits for other
cores.
.B map_at
installs one mapping at a given table level;
.B translate
and
.B permissions
read one back.
.B unmap_page
removes a mapping.
.B map_mmio
maps a physical range of device registers, uncached, and returns the
virtual address.
.B kernel_address_space
is the space every kernel thread runs in; every process's space maps the
same kernel half at the same addresses, so a kernel pointer is valid in
any of them.
See
.IR space (9)
for how a process's own half is built.
.B Vmm_Stats
reports the root table's address, how many frames are spent on tables
across every address space, bytes mapped, whether the no-execute bit and
global pages are available, and the largest leaf size the hardware
grants.
.SS The heap
.B heap.odin
is a slab allocator wired into
.B context.allocator
through
.BR allocator ,
so ordinary Odin
.B new
and
.B make
work once
.I kmain
installs it.
A small request, up to 2048 bytes, comes out of one of eight size
classes, each a chain of one-page slabs carved into equal objects
threaded through their own first eight bytes; allocation and free are a
pointer push and pop, with no search.
A larger request goes straight to the physical allocator as a run of
pages.
Both paths put a sixteen-byte header immediately before the returned
pointer, which is what lets
.B free
find which path a block came from with no separate lookup, and what
makes an over-aligned allocation possible: the block is oversized and the
header records how far in the returned pointer sits.
Slabs are never returned to the physical allocator; nothing in Vectra's
kernel heap yet needs the machinery that would prove a slab fully free.
.B Heap_Stats
reports each class's object size, free count and total, frames spent on
slabs, and the frame and block counts of large allocations.
.B live_objects
turns a
.B Heap_Stats
snapshot into one number: everything currently handed out and not yet
freed.
A self-test brackets a scratch allocation with two calls to it, and a
result other than zero is a leak.
.SH SOURCE
.B kernel/mem
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR arch (9),
.IR space (9),
.IR sync (9),
.IR testing (9)
.SH BUGS
A slab, once grown, is never shrunk, so a class that once held many
objects keeps its pages even after every object in it is freed.
